Positioning properties
======================
The CSS position property controls where an element appears. Its values are static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky. It works with top, right, bottom, and left. The z-index property controls which overlapping element appears on top.

Static: Default position. Follows normal page flow; top, right, bottom, left, and z-index do not apply.

Relative: Moves from its original position while preserving its original space. It can act as a reference for absolute children.

Absolute: Removed from normal flow and positioned relative to the nearest positioned parent.

Fixed: Positioned relative to the browser viewport and remains in place while scrolling.

Sticky: Behaves like relative positioning until a scroll point is reached, then sticks within its parent container.

Common pattern: Set the parent to position: relative and the child to position: absolute.

z-index: Controls which overlapping positioned element appears on top.

flex box
=========

CSS Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system used to arrange elements in a row or column. Apply display: flex to the parent, making its direct children flex items. flex-direction controls the main axis, justify-content aligns items along the main axis, and align-items aligns them along the cross axis. Use flex-wrap for responsive wrapping and gap for spacing. Flex-item properties such as flex-grow, flex-shrink, flex-basis, align-self, and order control individual items. Flexbox is commonly used for navigation bars, centered content, card layouts, equal columns, and sidebar layouts.


css responsive design common break points 
==========================================

/* Mobile */
@media (max-width: 576px) { }

/* Tablet */
@media (max-width: 768px) { }

/* Laptop */
@media (max-width: 992px) { }

/* Desktop */
@media (max-width: 1200px) { }



Media Query Syntax
====================
@media (min-width: 768px) { }

@media (max-width: 767px) { }

@media screen and (min-width: 768px) { }

